# ripgrep — read via $RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH, which .zshrc exports only if this
# file exists.
#
# Format is one argument per line. There is NO shell parsing: quotes, globs and
# spaces are taken literally, so `--glob=!.git/` needs no escaping and
# `--glob "!.git/"` would not work. Lines starting with `#` are ignored.
#
# These apply to every rg invocation, including the `grep` alias in .zshrc and
# anything shelling out to rg (fzf, nvim pickers). Override per-call with the
# opposite flag — e.g. `rg --case-sensitive`, `rg --no-hidden`.

# ── Matching ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lowercase patterns match case-insensitively; add a capital to go exact.
--smart-case

# ── What gets searched ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Search dotfiles and dot-directories — in a dotfiles repo the interesting
# content is mostly hidden by definition. .gitignore is still respected.
--hidden

# ...but never walk the object database. Without this, --hidden turns every
# search in a git repo into a scan of thousands of loose objects and packfiles.
--glob=!.git/

# Other high-volume directories that are rarely what you meant. These are
# usually covered by .gitignore, but not when searching outside a repo.
--glob=!node_modules/
--glob=!.venv/
--glob=!target/

# ── Output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Minified files and lockfiles produce single lines thousands of columns wide,
# which shreds the terminal. Truncate, but print a marker so a hit is never
# silently invisible.
--max-columns=200
--max-columns-preview
